<http://data.silknow.org/object/64af7c21-c894-3b0a-9ea6-45c5bad124b5/observation/2>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "In the early years of the 19th century boots gradually became acceptable for women. By 1804, half-boots with front lacing and ribbon trimmings, like this pair, had started to appear in fashion illustrations for 'walking' or 'morning' dress. Hardwearing cottons – the striped uppers are made of cotton jean – became increasingly available and were used as alternatives to leather. Heroines in novels by Jane Austen (1775-1817) are often described wearing footwear of this kind."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/64af7c21-c894-3b0a-9ea6-45c5bad124b5> .

