<http://data.silknow.org/object/64af7c21-c894-3b0a-9ea6-45c5bad124b5>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Blue and white striped cotton boot\nBritish, 1812 - 1820\nMuseum no. T.509-1913\nGiven by Messrs. Harrods Ltd. [2007]"@en , "Pair of women's striped cotton half-boots with a low heel and rounded toe"@en , "In the early years of the 19th century boots gradually became acceptable for women. By 1804, half-boots with front lacing and ribbon trimmings, like this pair, had started to appear in fashion illustrations for 'walking' or 'morning' dress. Hardwearing cottons – the striped uppers are made of cotton jean – became increasingly available and were used as alternatives to leather. Heroines in novels by Jane Austen (1775-1817) are often described wearing footwear of this kind."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/35a16934-808d-3687-a9c2-0cade7e25113> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/5467521a-52fd-377e-89d4-49389963af3b> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "In the early years of the 19th century boots gradually became acceptable for women. By 1804, half-boots with front lacing and ribbon trimmings, like this pair, had started to appear in fashion illustrations for 'walking' or 'morning' dress. Hardwearing cottons – the striped uppers are made of cotton jean – became increasingly available and were used as alternatives to leather. Heroines in novels by Jane Austen (1775-1817) are often described wearing footwear of this kind."@en , "Blue and white striped cotton boot\nBritish, 1812 - 1820\nMuseum no. T.509-1913\nGiven by Messrs. Harrods Ltd. [2007]"@en , "Pair of women's striped cotton half-boots with a low heel and rounded toe"@en ;
